    "body": "EICMA 2026 • 2026 EICMA's Speed Legends Exhibit Was Not To Be Missed – See the Bikes That Paved MotoGP History\nFuture Artemis Spacesuit Gets Sortie Twin, ISS Test Mission Being Planned\n10 photos\nPhoto: Axiom Space\nWith all the things that are happening with the Artemis Moon exploration program, from the shift to the creation of a lunar base to the upcoming Artemis II mission, I kind of lost sight of the fact that a lot of these upcoming efforts will require a brand-new spacesuit to be realized. Luckily, those working on this new tech keep us updated from time to time.\nThat would be private space company Axiom , whose Extravehicular Mobility Unit (AxEMU) just reached a major milestone in its development: it passed the critical design review (CDR) and is now ready to move into production and qualification testing.\nThe company made the first steps toward this milestone in the summer of last year, when it delivered the first components to NASA. Since then, it has been hard at work testing everything, including components, engineering, and flight support equipment.\nUp next, Axiom will start qualification testing, which requires it to perform, among other things, environmental, vibration, and life-cycle testing. If all goes well here, the path to certification is open.\nAxiom plans to have the pressure garment and portable life support system ready by early next year, as it moves to performing a real-world demonstration of the suit on the International Space Station (ISS) at a yet undisclosed time.\nSomewhat behind closed doors, the private space company seems to have been working on what may very well be considered a scaled-down version of the suit. They call it the Sortie suit, or AxEMU-S1, and it has been imagined for use during short-duration lunar surface excursions.\nMeant to support the early crewed Artemis missions to the Moon starting in 2028, it uses certified AxEMU components, meaning Axiom will be capable of producing it in parallel to the main suit.\nThe suits are part of NASA's Exploration Extravehicular Mobility Unit (xEMU) program, which kicked off back in 2019 as a means to come up with the first major spacesuit redesign since the 1980s.\nWhen ready, the protective hardware will be able to withstand the thermal extremes on the Moon (between minus 250 degrees Fahrenheit in the shade and up to 250 degrees in the sun) and survive micrometeoroid impact.\nNASA also requested that the suit have resistance to the abrasive lunar dust to avoid the issues experienced during the Apollo program – back then, exposure to the lunar dust ate entire layers. Unlike existing designs, which use snoopy cap headsets for comms, the new suit will also be equipped with voice-activated microphones.\nAlthough Axiom is the main suit supplier for the Artemis missions, another company, Collins Aerospace, is working on its own design as well, derived from one that was initially intended to replace the ones currently in use on the ISS. Sadly, Collins hasn't updated us on how things are going on this front in a very long time.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nAxEMU NASA spacesuit Axiom Space Axiom\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT All 2026 Formula 1 Drivers Can Now Be Had as LEGO Minifigures for the First Time, Race Cars in Tow",
